Subject: [RUSTSEC-2026-0042] Typosquat of 'smtp-pool' crate with malicious build script
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

Dear crate maintainer,

A typosquatted crate named `smtp-pool\u202e` (with an appended Unicode U+202E Right-to-Left Override character) was published to crates.io on October 8, 2026. This crate is visually identical to the legitimate `smtp-pool` crate but contains a malicious `build.rs` script.

Technical details:
- The malicious crate name contains U+202E (RIGHT-TO-LEFT OVERRIDE), making it appear identical to `smtp-pool` in most terminals and editors
- The build.rs script downloads and executes a payload from an external server
- Downloaded 4,219 times before removal from the index
- CVSS 3.1: 9.8 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H)

Your crate `tower-lsp` lists `smtp-pool = "0.4"` as a dependency. If your users ran `cargo update` after October 8, they may have resolved to the malicious version.

What to do:
1. Pin to the known-safe version in your Cargo.toml:
     [dependencies]
     smtp-pool = "=0.4.2"
2. Check your local cache:
     ls ~/.cargo/registry/cache/*/smtp-pool-0.4.3*
   If present, delete and run `cargo clean`.
3. Verify with:
     cargo tree -d | grep smtp-pool

Registry status: The malicious crate has been removed and the publisher account revoked. Cached copies may persist in CI build caches.

